<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<section xml:id="sec_6-4_Inverse-Trigonometry" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Section 6.4: Inverse Trigonometric Functions and Their Derivatives</title>

  <slide>
    <title>Learning Goals</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>Use inverse trigonometric functions to find multiple solutions to trigonometric equations.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Evaluate composition of trigonometric functions and inverse trigonometric functions.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>Calculate derivatives of inverse trigonometric functions.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Restricted Domains</title>
    
    <p>
      Inverse functions allow us to solve equations <m>f(x)=y</m>, <m>x=f^{-1}(y)</m>.
      This only works if for a given value of <m>y</m> there is only <em>one</em> value <m>x</m> making <m>f(x)=y</m> true.
      Otherwise, <m>f^{-1}</m> wouldn't be a function.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because trigonometric functions are periodic, they are not one-to-one unless we <term>restrict</term> the domain:
      <md>
        <mrow> y = \sin(x) \amp: \quad x \in [-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}], \; y \in [-1,1] </mrow>
        <mrow> y = \cos(x) \amp: \quad x \in [0, \pi], \; y \in [-1,1] </mrow>
        <mrow> y = \tan(x) \amp: \quad x \in (-\frac{\pi}{2}, \frac{\pi}{2}), \; y \in (-\infty, \infty) </mrow>
        <mrow> y = \sec(x) \amp: \quad x \in [0, \frac{\pi}{2}) \cup ( \frac{\pi}{2}, \pi], \; y \in (-\infty, -1] \cup [1, \infty) </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
    <image width="50%">
      <shortdescription>Unit circles emphasizing the restricted domains for trigonometric functions</shortdescription>
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}
          [
          xmin=-1.5,
          xmax=10.5,
          ymin=-1.5,
          ymax=1.5,
          height=2in,
          width=6in,
          axis lines=none, ticks=none
          ]
          \addplot[black] coordinates {(-1.1,0) (1.1,0)};
          \addplot[black] coordinates {(0, -1.1) (0, 1.1)} node [pos=0, below] {$\sin(x)$};
          \addplot [domain=0:1, samples=200, black] ({0+cos(deg(2*pi*\x))},{sin(deg(2*pi*\x))});
          \addplot [domain=-0.5:0.5, samples=200, blue, very thick, 
                    mark=*, mark options={fill=blue, fill opacity=1}, mark indices={1,200}]
                    ({0+cos(deg(pi*\x))},{sin(deg(pi*\x))});
          \addplot[black] coordinates {(1.4,0) (3.6,0)};
          \addplot[black] coordinates {(2.5, -1.1) (2.5, 1.1)} node [pos=0, below] {$\cos(x)$};;
          \addplot [domain=0:1, samples=200, black] ({2.5+cos(deg(2*pi*\x))},{sin(deg(2*pi*\x))});
          \addplot [domain=0:1, samples=200, blue, very thick, 
                    mark=*, mark options={fill=blue, fill opacity=1}, mark indices={1,200}]
                   ({2.5+cos(deg(pi*\x))},{sin(deg(pi*\x))});
          \addplot[black] coordinates {(3.9,0) (6.1,0)};
          \addplot[black] coordinates {(5, -1.1) (5, 1.1)} node [pos=0, below] {$\tan(x)$};;
          \addplot [domain=0:1, samples=200, black] ({5+cos(deg(2*pi*\x))},{sin(deg(2*pi*\x))});
          \addplot [domain=-0.5:0.5, samples=200, blue, very thick, 
                    mark=*, mark options={fill=white, fill opacity=1}, mark indices={1,200}]
                   ({5+cos(deg(pi*\x))},{sin(deg(pi*\x))});
          \addplot[black] coordinates {(6.4,0) (8.6,0)};
          \addplot[black] coordinates {(7.5, -1.1) (7.5, 1.1)} node [pos=0, below] {$\sec(x)$};;
          \addplot [domain=0:1, samples=200, black] ({7.5+cos(deg(2*pi*\x))},{sin(deg(2*pi*\x))});
          \addplot [domain=0:1, samples=200, blue, very thick, 
                    mark=*, mark options={fill=blue, fill opacity=1}, mark indices={1,200}]
                   ({7.5+cos(deg(pi*\x))},{sin(deg(pi*\x))});
          \addplot [mark=*, mark options={blue, fill=white}, very thick] coordinates {(7.5,1)};
          \end{axis}
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Inverse Trigonometric Functions</title>
    
    <p>
      Trigonometric functions have <em>angles</em> as input and <em>values</em> as output.
      Inverse trigonometric functions take <em>values</em> as input and <em>angles</em> as output.
    </p>
    <p>
      Interpret the following:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>\sin^{-1}(0.5)</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\cos^{-1}(\frac{2}{3})</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\tan^{-1}(-3)</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Solving Trigonometric Equations</title>
    
    <p>
      Solve the equations. Find multiple solutions.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>3 \sin(x) + 1 = 0</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\sin(x) - 3\cos(x) = 0</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Composition of Functions</title>
    
    <p>
      When evaluating composition of trigonometric functions and inverse trigonometric functions,
      it is critical to think about what the input of a function represents<mdash/>an angle or a value<mdash/>and what interval is relevant.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>\sin^{-1}(\sin(\frac{\pi}{4}))</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\cos^{-1}(\cos(\frac{-3\pi}{4}))</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\tan(\tan^{-1}(3))</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\tan^{-1}(\tan(\frac{13\pi}{4}))</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Composition of Functions, continued</title>
    
    <p>
      The Pythagorean identities help us when working with composition of inverse of paired functions.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>\cos(\sin^{-1}(\frac{3}{4}))</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>\sec(\tan^{-1}(4))</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>Implicit Function Derivative</title>
    
    <p>
      If we want to find the derivative of <m>y = \sin^{-1}(x)</m>, use implicit differentiation on the inverse equation
      <md>
        x = \sin(y)
      </md>
      and get
      <md>
        1 = \cos(y) y' \qquad \Rightarrow \qquad y' = \frac{1}{\cos(y)} = \frac{1}{\cos(\sin^{-1}(x))}
      </md>
      Now we use the Pythagorean identity to simplify:
      <md>
        \frac{d}{dx}\Big[\sin^{-1}(x)\Big] = \mathrm{asin}'(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}
      </md>      
    </p>
  </slide>

  <slide>
    <title>All Implicit Function Derivatives</title>
    
    <md>
      <mrow>\mathrm{asin}'(x) \amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</mrow>
      <mrow>\mathrm{acos}'(x) \amp = \frac{-1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</mrow>
      <mrow>\mathrm{atan}'(x) \amp = \frac{1}{x^2+1}</mrow>
      <mrow>\mathrm{asec}'(x) \amp = \frac{1}{|x|\sqrt{x^2-1}}</mrow>
    </md>
    <p>
      Find <m>f'(x)</m> for the following functions:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p><m>f(x) = \mathrm{asin}(x^2)</m></p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><m>f(x) = \tan^{-1}(e^{2x})</m></p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </slide>
</section>
